Separate a molecular conformation from a drawing and a physical pathway (@chemistry-conformations: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Test-spatial-identity-using-proper-rotations-and-ligand-labels.lingua#r_00978F6428EF14A811F9486FF4") { r_00A70A2D6280013B3B40B03DDC
The graph tells us what is connected; it does not fix all distances and angles. A molecular conformation is a spatial arrangement within a specified connectivity and bonding framework. Bond lengths, bond angles and torsional or dihedral angles describe different geometric freedoms. A four-atom sequence A–B–C–D defines two planes, ABC and BCD; their relative angle around BC is a dihedral when both planes are well-defined. Collinear triples make that particular plane-based descriptor degenerate.

A Newman projection views along a selected bond, placing its front and rear atoms on the same line of sight. It records substituent directions around that bond. Declare which atom is in front; changing the viewpoint changes the drawing convention. Rotating the whole molecule preserves all its internal distances and dihedrals. Rotating one part about an internal bond generally changes some of them. These operations must not be confused even though both can make a diagram look different.

An original four-point model makes the distinction exact. Put B=(0,0,0), C=(1,0,0), A=(0,1,0) and D=(1,cos φ,sin φ). AB, BC and CD all have length one, and the two adjacent bond angles remain right angles. Yet |A−D|²=1+(cos φ−1)²+sin²φ=3−2cos φ. At φ=0 the squared separation is one; at φ=π it is five. This is a geometric illustration of internal torsion, not a proposed equilibrium geometry or force field for a real molecule. A rigid rotation of all four points could not change that separation.

Read [Organic Chemistry tenth edition by John McMurry](../sources/Organic-Chemistry-tenth-edition-by-John-McMurry.lingua#r_00F02946DB1C7E37631D69129A), sections 3.6–3.7, for ethane and butane conformational descriptions. The actual butane passage distinguishes anti and gauche minima and higher-energy eclipsed arrangements; its simplified values are model/experimental teaching information, not an instruction to assign the same energy to every similar-looking molecule. Rotation about a single bond need not be energetically free, and a low-energy conformer is not automatically the only populated one. Populations require a thermodynamic account; interconversion rates require kinetic information. Reuse the earlier chemical energy and rate lessons for that distinction.

A continuous geometric path does not by itself prove that a molecular rearrangement is dynamically accessible on an observation timescale. A barrier, ring constraint, surrounding medium or electronic change can matter. Conversely an observed average structure need not be a geometry occupied by every molecule. If a hypothetical population equally occupies the φ=0 and φ=π states above, the mean position of D is (1,0,0), which coincides with C and has lost the fixed CD length. Averaging coordinates and calculating a bond length do not commute. This counterexample warns against interpreting an average drawing as an instantaneous molecule.

Conformational changes, configurational changes, resonance contributors and sample mixtures therefore need separate accounts. Resonance changes an electronic representation, not the nuclear coordinates through an assumed time trajectory. A mixture contains amounts of different constituents; a sequence of images may instead follow one entity over time. State the time and resolution of the observation before inferring separability or a single fixed structure. Full molecular dynamics, transition-state theory and conformational ensembles remain later formation.

**Work:** derive the four-point distance formula and the averaging counterexample, then redraw the same geometry from the opposite bond direction. Explain which geometrical invariants distinguish internal rotation from whole-object rotation. Critique a claim that one low-energy picture proves both a unique molecular geometry and a fast physical pathway. Use supplied geometries and published discussion; no molecular simulation or physical experiment is claimed.

**Continue:** [Test spatial identity using proper rotations and ligand labels](Test-spatial-identity-using-proper-rotations-and-ligand-labels.lingua#r_00978F6428EF14A811F9486FF4).

